{"units": [{"id": "ochthe-note2", "translation": "Similar to this word is what is read in M: ἐπ᾽ ὄχθει ἄξεν [on the bank he broke], an obvious corruption. For the meter and the sense require a verb such as ἐπορθιάζων [raising the voice] or ἐπορθιᾷ [he raises the voice], which are uncertain conjectures of Paleius and Weilius."}, {"id": "ochthe-quote1", "translation": "creep along the banks of this river"}, {"id": "ochthe-quote2", "translation": "by the river banks"}, {"id": "ochthe-quote3", "translation": "around Cocytus and the Acherusian banks"}, {"id": "ochthe-word1", "translation": "and Acherusian"}, {"id": "ochthe-word2", "translation": "banks"}, {"id": "ochthe-word3", "translation": "on the bank he broke"}, {"id": "ochthe-word4", "translation": "raising the voice"}, {"id": "ochthe-word5", "translation": "he raises the voice"}, {"id": "ochthos-orth1", "translation": "mound, bank"}, {"id": "ochthos-def1", "translation": "mound"}, {"id": "ochthos-def2", "translation": "burial mound"}, {"id": "ochthos-note1", "translation": "Used in the sense of 'bank' at Ag. 1161, on which see under ὄχθη."}, {"id": "ochthos-quote1", "translation": "a high mound near the sea"}, {"id": "ochthos-quote2", "translation": "the dear man, the dear mound"}, {"id": "ochthos-quote3", "translation": "come to the topmost peak of the mound"}, {"id": "ochthos-quote4", "translation": "on the mound of the tomb"}, {"id": "ochthos-word1", "translation": "banks"}, {"id": "ochthos-word2", "translation": "bank"}, {"id": "ochleo-orth1", "translation": "to trouble, annoy"}, {"id": "ochleo-def1", "translation": "to trouble, annoy"}, {"id": "ochleo-quote1", "translation": "you trouble me in vain"}, {"id": "ochlos-orth1", "translation": "crowd, multitude; trouble, annoyance"}, {"id": "ochlos-def1", "translation": "crowd, multitude of people"}, {"id": "ochlos-def2", "translation": "trouble, annoyance"}, {"id": "ochlos-note1", "translation": "Of a troublesome narrative"}, {"id": "ochlos-note2", "translation": "Of the trouble of labors"}, {"id": "ochlos-note3", "translation": "ibid."}, {"id": "ochlos-note4", "translation": "Thus rightly Doederlein for χόλον."}, {"id": "ochlos-quote1", "translation": "a tower keeps off the crowd of enemies"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote2", "translation": "and a crowd of Lydians, living in luxury, follows"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote3", "translation": "a mixed crowd"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote4", "translation": "where is the other crowd of friends?"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote5", "translation": "I see a crowd of shield-bearers and spear-shakers"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote6", "translation": "I will omit the greater part of the crowd of words"}, {"id": "ochlos-quote7", "translation": "so that the present crowd of troubles seems to you a game"}, {"id": "ochlos-word1", "translation": "anger"}, {"id": "ochmazo-orth1", "translation": "to fasten, bind"}, {"id": "ochmazo-def1", "translation": "to fasten (for the purpose of detaining or restraining)"}, {"id": "ochmazo-quote1", "translation": "to fasten this villain to the high-cliffed rocks"}, {"id": "ochmazo-quote2", "translation": "tell who fastened you in the ravine"}, {"id": "ochos-orth1", "translation": "chariot, vehicle"}, {"id": "ochos-def1", "translation": "chariot, vehicle"}, {"id": "ochos-def2", "translation": "Improperly said of a ship by which one is carried"}, {"id": "ochos-note1", "translation": "κώλοις Wakefield"}, {"id": "ochos-quote1", "translation": "I rushed shoeless on a winged chariot"}, {"id": "ochos-quote2", "translation": "they dwell aloft - on well-wheeled chariots"}, {"id": "ochos-quote3", "translation": "having desolated this chariot"}, {"id": "ochos-quote4", "translation": "having yoked this chariot with vigorous colts"}, {"id": "ochos-quote5", "translation": "send with a swift chariot to the sea"}, {"id": "ochos-word1", "translation": "limbs"}, {"id": "ochyros-orth1", "translation": "firm, strong"}, {"id": "ochyros-def1", "translation": "firm"}, {"id": "ochyros-note1", "translation": "See ἐχυρός."}, {"id": "ochyros-word1", "translation": "firm, secure"}, {"id": "ops-vox-orth1", "translation": "voice"}, {"id": "ops-vox-def1", "translation": "voice"}, {"id": "ops-vox-quote1", "translation": "one will think he hears the voice of the wife of Tereus"}, {"id": "opsanon-orth1", "translation": "sight, vision"}, {"id": "opsanon-def1", "translation": "sight, vision"}, {"id": "opsanon-note1", "translation": "where I corrected with Martin to ἂν τόδ᾽ ὄψανον πέλοι [would this sight be]."}, {"id": "opsanon-quote1", "translation": "not in vain is the sight of a man"}, {"id": "opsanon-word1", "translation": "would this sight be"}, {"id": "opse-orth1", "translation": "late"}, {"id": "opse-def1", "translation": "late"}, {"id": "opse-quote1", "translation": "you will learn, taught late at least, to be wise"}, {"id": "opsigonos-orth1", "translation": "late-born"}, {"id": "opsigonos-def1", "translation": "late-born"}, {"id": "opsigonos-note1", "translation": "Of a young man"}, {"id": "opsigonos-quote1", "translation": "but you, old in mind, learn from one late-born"}, {"id": "opsikoitos-orth1", "translation": "late-sleeping"}, {"id": "opsikoitos-def1", "translation": "late-sleeping"}, {"id": "opsikoitos-quote1", "translation": "and in late-sleeping eyes I have injuries"}, {"id": "opsis-orth1", "translation": "sight, appearance; spectacle; vision"}, {"id": "opsis-def1", "translation": "sight, appearance"}, {"id": "opsis-def2", "translation": "spectacle"}, {"id": "opsis-def3", "translation": "vision, aspect"}, {"id": "opsis-note1", "translation": "Suspected is what is read in Hesychius: δίκρα ὄψις: ἡ διπλῆ. Αἰσχύλος Τροφοῖς [two-pronged sight: the double. Aeschylus in Nurses] (fr. 47), where M. Schmidt conjectured δίκρα ὀσφῦς [two-pronged loins]: on which I have spoken under δίκρος."}, {"id": "opsis-quote1", "translation": "nightly visions"}, {"id": "opsis-quote2", "translation": "visions of dream phantoms"}, {"id": "opsis-quote3", "translation": "O vision of night, like to dreams"}, {"id": "opsis-quote4", "translation": "the vision has gone, not later"}, {"id": "opsis-quote5", "translation": "a fearful sight to behold"}, {"id": "opsis-quote6", "translation": "an unusual sight"}, {"id": "opsis-quote7", "translation": "a bottomless sight"}, {"id": "opsis-quote8", "translation": "now having a reversed sight in prayers"}, {"id": "opsis-quote9", "translation": "it seemed to me that two well-dressed women came into sight"}, {"id": "opsis-quote10", "translation": "you have come into the sight of those whom you long ago boasted of"}, {"id": "opsis-word1", "translation": "two-pronged sight: the double. Aeschylus in Nurses"}, {"id": "opsis-word2", "translation": "two-pronged loins"}, {"id": "opsis-word3", "translation": "two-pronged"}, {"id": "opson-orth1", "translation": "food, relish"}, {"id": "opson-def1", "translation": "food, relish"}, {"id": "opson-quote1", "translation": "for what food could be better for a man than this?"}, {"id": "prosthroneo-orth1", "translation": "to address, speak to"}, {"id": "prosthroneo-def1", "translation": "to address, speak to"}, {"id": "prosthroneo-quote1", "translation": "who then, O wretched one, addresses me, the miserable one, so truly?"}, {"id": "stomargos-orth1", "translation": "loud-mouthed, brawling"}, {"id": "stomargos-def1", "translation": "loud-mouthed, brawling"}, {"id": "stomargos-note1", "translation": "which the scholiast explains as μανιώδης τὴν γλῶσσαν [mad in tongue]."}, {"id": "stomargos-quote1", "translation": "even if he is too loud-mouthed"}, {"id": "stomargos-word1", "translation": "mad in tongue"}]}